      "content": "For tempering 70% dark chocolate, a typical temperature curve involves three main phases: melting, seeding, and setting. Start by melting the chocolate to around 115-120°F, then cool it to around 82-85°F while agitating to achieve the \"seeding\" phase. Next, reheat the chocolate to 86-88°F to achieve the desired \"bloomless\" or \"stable\" temper, where the cocoa butter crystals form a stable crystal structure. This final phase typically takes about 10-15 minutes.",
